left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">OpenTimelineIO (OTIO)</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">OpenTimelineIO is maintained by the Academy Software Foundation (AcademySoftwareFoundation/OpenTimelineIO) under the Apache License 2.0 as an open interchange standard for media production editorial workflows. Constructed with a C++ core wrapped in comprehensive Python bindings, OTIO provides a standardized data model for representing timelines, tracks, clips, gaps, transitions, and markers. The library serializes into human-readable JSON formats and allows custom metadata dictionaries to be attached to individual clips or markers. It acts as a semantic bridge that allows language models and human editors to collaborate within a shared environment without requiring pixel processing or video rendering. Within this architecture, OTIO serves as the primary schema for scene contracts and shot lists.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Screenplain and fountain-tools</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Screenplay parsing is addressed by open-source plain-text parsers, specifically screenplain (developed by Martin Vilcans) and fountain-tools / fountain-py (developed by Ian Thomas). Released under the MIT license, these lightweight Python utilities convert plain-text Fountain markup into structured object graphs. The resulting object graph classifies text blocks into distinct screenplay element types, including scene headings, action descriptions, character names, parentheticals, dialogue lines, and transition markers. Recent updates to screenplain incorporate modern Python packaging standards (uv, pyproject.toml). These parsers enable Hermes to ingest scripts formatted in plain text and decompose them into structured scene elements.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Outlines</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Outlines (dottxt-ai/outlines) is an open-source library released under the Apache License 2.0 designed to enforce structured generation from language models. Rather than relying on post-generation parsing or prompt engineering, Outlines operates at the inference layer by constructing finite state machines (FSMs) and regex automata that mask syntactically illegal tokens during model decoding. It integrates natively with Hugging Face transformers, vllm, llama.cpp, and cloud providers like OpenAI. By guaranteeing strict adherence to Pydantic models, JSON schemas, or regular expressions without adding inference latency, Outlines eliminates structural hallucinations and formatting failures in local and hosted generation workflows.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Instructor and Pydantic V2</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Instructor (jxnl/instructor) and Pydantic V2 (pydantic/pydantic) provide data validation and structured extraction capabilities under the MIT license. Pydantic leverages compiled Rust binaries (pydantic-core) to deliver type validation, automatic type coercion, and JSON schema generation. Instructor extends Pydantic by managing validation retries; when a model output fails schema validation, Instructor catches the validation error and sends a targeted re-prompt containing the validation feedback back to the model. This pattern provides a secondary validation layer when operating with cloud LLM endpoints that do not support token-level FSM masking.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">CrewAI Multi-Agent Framework</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">CrewAI (crewAIInc/crewAI) is an open-source Python framework designed for orchestrating autonomous, role-based multi-agent teams. Built around event-driven task routing and CrewAI Flows, the framework allows developers to define specialized agents using declarative YAML configurations or Python code. CrewAI supports native Model Context Protocol (MCP) integrations, persistent memory systems, checkpointing, and asynchronous task execution. Benchmarks indicate that CrewAI exhibits low orchestration overhead (200‚Äì400ms latency) and supports up to 100 concurrent agent workflows. Within Hermes, CrewAI Flows manage asynchronous background creative tasks, such as parallel draft editing or claim verification.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">DeepEval and DSPy</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">DeepEval (confident-ai/deepeval) and DSPy (stanfordnlp/dspy) provide evaluation and constraint enforcement for language model applications under the Apache 2.0 and MIT licenses, respectively. DeepEval integrates with Pytest to deliver over 50 evaluation metrics, including G-Eval, factual consistency, hallucination detection, and claim verification. DSPy shifts language model programming away from manual prompting by compiling prompts against declarative assertions, dynamically enforcing structural and logical rules at runtime. Together, these frameworks supply the verification layer required to validate script factual consistency and narrative flow.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">4. Script to Scene Ecosystem Findings</span></h2><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Research Area 1 ‚Äî Idea and Content Angle Generation</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Topic discovery and content angle selection are executed by synthesizing research intelligence gathered during preceding research passes . Candidate capabilities operate through Hermes web research tools and Gemini integration . Ideation workflows evaluate novelty, audience hook potential, and content framing by feeding research evidence chunks into structured ideation prompts . By enforcing structured Pydantic schemas, Hermes outputs a set of creative angle hypotheses containing target demographics, premise framing, primary conflict, and format selections (such as documentary deep-dives, video essays, or commentary).</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Research Area 2 ‚Äî Hook Generation</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hook generation focuses on structuring the crucial opening moments of a video. Capabilities combine documented media heuristics (including visual cold opens, curiosity gaps, question hooks, tension setups, and narrative promises) with model-generated creative variants. Rather than relying on unverified claims regarding retention guarantees, the framework treats hook variants as testable narrative hypotheses. Hermes evaluates candidate hooks using prompt-based audience simulation skills and deterministic length constraints to ensure the core premise is established within the initial thirty-second window.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Research Area 3 ‚Äî Story Narrative Structure</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Story structuring translates selected angles into complete narrative arcs. Supported structures include traditional three-act arcs, investigative documentary frameworks, problem-solution-impact explainers, and chronological deep dives. These structural patterns do not require custom narrative engines; they are represented as declarative configuration templates within CrewAI Flows or Hermes narrative Skills. The framework models narrative escalation, open loops, setup-and-payoff pairs, and tension-release cycles by generating structured outline objects prior to full script drafting.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Research Area 4 ‚Äî Script Generation</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Script generation produces fully articulated narrative copy. The framework uses plain-text Fountain markup as the intermediate script representation. Fountain is human-readable, lightweight, and parsed into structured data objects by Python utilities like screenplain and fountain-tools. The script generation pipeline accommodates long-form essays, short-form scripts, commentary, and scene-linked voiceover tracks. Revision workflows operate on discrete Fountain elements, preserving narrative continuity across multi-pass edits without corrupting document structure. The workflow progresses from raw Fountain text to an AST, which is then transformed into OpenTimelineIO tracks and clips before final validation:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_1-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Raw Fountain Text Input</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Language models generate plain-text scripts incorporating Fountain syntax rules.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">AST Transformation</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: fountain-tools converts the raw plain text into an abstract syntax tree composed of scene headings, action blocks, and dialogue elements.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Timeline Mapping</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: A Hermes skill maps AST elements to OpenTimelineIO tracks, clips, and markers .</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Contract Schema Validation</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Outlines or Pydantic validates the resulting object graph against the binding production contract.</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Research Area 5 ‚Äî Fact-Grounded Scripting</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Fact grounding preserves source lineage across the creative pipeline . Research evidence snippets are assigned unique, immutable source identifiers during initial discovery . When generating script copy, Hermes requires the language model to attach claim metadata and source references to corresponding script statements.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The lineage model tracks information flow from origin to execution: a unique research source identifier links to an extracted factual claim node, which connects to supporting evidence passages, and ultimately attaches to a specific Fountain script statement as an inline comment (e.g., /* Source: SRC-082 */). This mechanism maintains complete provenance from raw research data to spoken voiceover text. Automated claim verification utilities evaluate script statements against source context using DeepEval factual consistency metrics to flag unsupported assertions prior to production.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Research Area 6 ‚Äî Script Analysis</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Script analysis combines deterministic calculation with language model reasoning to evaluate completed drafts. Deterministic analyzers compute quantitative metrics, including total word count, estimated reading duration, repetition density, visual-to-narration ratio, and scene transition frequency. Concurrently, Hermes evaluation skills analyze qualitative dimensions, including narrative pacing, hook strength, contextual clarity, structural escalation, and logical coherence.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Research Area 7 ‚Äî Script-to-Scene Decomposition</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Script-to-scene decomposition transforms linear script text into structured scene representations. Using Python Fountain parsers, the system splits scripts along scene headings (INT., EXT.) and element boundaries. Each decomposed scene node is populated with specific metadata:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_2-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Narrative Boundaries</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Scene identifiers, narrative purpose, and sequence boundaries.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Audio Intent</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Exact spoken voiceover text, tone directions, and sound effects (SFX) requirements.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Visual Intent</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Visual subject descriptions, camera framing (e.g., wide shot, close-up), motion vectors, and visual cues.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Asset Requirements</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Categorized asset requirements identifying b-roll, archival footage, motion graphics, 2D/3D animation, generative AI video, or stock photography .</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Research Area 8 ‚Äî Structured Scene Contract</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The output of scene decomposition is formalized as a rigid, Pydantic-validated Scene Contract. This contract acts as the interface between creative planning and downstream media generation systems.</span></p><table style="border-spacing:0;border-collapse:collapse;margin-right:auto"><thead><tr style="height:0pt"><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:156pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span st